\cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 Hope your move went smoothly and you are enjoying being back in the US.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 I have returned to the complex contour project, and I now have hope that it might work!\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 My progress\'a0is\'a0realising we can\'a0do\'a0a\'a0singular\'a0regular split of the first-order field in the complex plane.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 Therefore, I think we\'a0will be able to\'a0solve the second-order problem by separating the second-order source into a singular-singular (SS) piece, a singular-regular (SR) piece, and a regular-regular (RR) piece.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 The SS piece can be solved as we originally solved the 1/\\rho^4 source integral we originally started with by using the 3D formulas (no mode decomposition of the first-order field)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 I think the SR piece can be solved similarly by calculating the source using the 3D expressions for the singular piece (no first-order mode decomposition) and a first-order mode decomposition for the\'a0R\'a0piece.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 I think the RR source can be calculated using the first-order mode decomposition. However, I'm not sure if this will work on the contour because you may get awkward products of complex functions that infect the real solution. Alternatively, we could solve the RR piece on the real line, which should be fine.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 In the attached notebook, I demonstrate how the first-order field can be split into a singular and regular field on the complex\'a0contour:\cb1 \
\cb3 I employ a worldtube around the particle.\'a0\
Outside the worldtube, I solve the usual Poisson equation on the real line.\'a0\
I inject a homogeneous solution with boundary conditions on the left-hand side to act as a regular field.\'a0\cb1 \
\cb3 In the worldtube, I solve along a\'a0contour.\'a0I solve for the full field with the extended Dirac Delta as a source (for comparison purposes). I also solve for the regular field\'a0with an effective source, where\'a0I\'a0use an expansion of\'a0the singular field and\'a0take away\'a0the corresponding source from the extended Dirac delta function to calculate the effective source.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 The plots compare the results, and I find I can solve for the regular part of the field inside the contour\'a0to a\'a0good accuracy, which is very cool!\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 You can change the value of l,\'a0the amplitude and size of the worldtube\'a0and the\'a0order of the singular field expansion.\cb1 \
\
\cb3 Let me know what you think, and if you would like to meet so I can explain it further.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
